<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Ram Khamhaeng</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1237–<a href="/years/1298">1298</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Ram Khamhaeng the Great</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Ram Khamhaeng was the third king of Sukhothai, reigning from about 1279 to 1298, under whom the Thai kingdom expanded widely. Tradition credits him with creating the Thai script, recorded in the Ram Khamhaeng inscription. He was an ally of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> of Lan Na and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ngam-mueang">Ngam Mueang</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and is said to have helped found Chiang Mai in 1296.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ram-khamhaeng.jpg" alt="Ram Khamhaeng" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: This Photo was taken by Supanut Arunoprayote. … · CC BY 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ram_Khamhaeng_the_Great_(I).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ramkhamhaeng was a son of Pho Khun Bang Klang Hao, who ruled as Pho Khun Si Inthrathit, and his queen Sueang, though folk legend gives his parents as an ogress named Kangli and a fisherman. He had two brothers and two sisters. At 19 he took part in his father's successful campaign to capture the city of Sukhothai, formerly a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vassal">vassal</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer Empire</a>, which established the kingdom's independence. For his courage in battle he was reportedly granted the title Phra Ram Khamhaeng, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rama">Rama</a> the Bold.</p><p>His elder brother <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ban-mueang">Ban Mueang</a> became king after their father's death and appointed him ruler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/si-satchanalai">Si Satchanalai</a>. Ramkhamhaeng succeeded Ban Mueang. The historian Tri Amattayakun suggests he acceded in 1279, the year he planted a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> palm in Sukhothai.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>The most significant event at the start of his reign was the elopement of one of his daughters with the captain of the palace guards, a commoner. The commoner went on to found the Burmese Hanthawaddy kingdom and to commission the Code of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wareru">Wareru</a>, which became a basis for the law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thailand">Thailand</a> used in Siam until 1908.</p><p>Ramkhamhaeng sent embassies to Yuan China from 1282 to 1323 and imported the techniques of making the ceramics known as Sangkhalok ware, long a major export of Sukhothai. He had close relations with the rulers of nearby cities, especially Ngammueang of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phayao">Phayao</a> and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mangrai">Mangrai</a> of Chiang Mai. His campaign against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambodia">Cambodia</a> left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khmer-empire">Khmer</a> country utterly devastated. Tradition credits him with creating the Thai alphabet from Khmer, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a>, Pali and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grantha-script">Grantha scripts</a>, and with firmly establishing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a> as the state religion.</p><p>It is speculated that his kingdom reached Lampang, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phrae">Phrae</a> and Nan in the north, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phitsanulok">Phitsanulok</a> and Vientiane in the east, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nakhon-si-thammarat">Nakhon Si Thammarat</a> in the south and Mon kingdoms in the west. Yet in the mandala model of politics, kingdoms such as Sukhothai had no distinct borders and were centred on the strength of the capital, so claims of a large kingdom served to assert Siamese dominance over mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/southeast-asia">Southeast Asia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>According to the Chinese History of Yuan, Ramkhamhaeng died in 1298 and was succeeded by his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loe-thai">Loe Thai</a>. Legend holds that he perished in the rapids of the rivers of Sawankhalok.</p><p>Much of the traditional information about him comes from the Ramkhamhaeng stele, composed in 1292, which holds vague facts about the king. It is kept in the Bangkok National Museum and was added to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a>'s Memory of the World Register in 2003. Ramkhamhaeng University, the first Thai university with an open-door policy, is named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Ramkhamhaeng” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tradition holds that he chose the site in consultation with his allies Ngam Mueang of Phayao and <b>Ram Khamhaeng of Sukhothai</b>, with whom he had sworn friendship.</blockquote><a href="/empire/lan-na">Lan Na · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Ram Khamhaeng</b>'s inscription says a bell hung at the palace gate so that any subject with a grievance could ring it and be heard by the king.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sukhothai-kingdom">Sukhothai Kingdom · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>Its best-known ruler, Ngam Mueang, was an ally of Mangrai of Lan Na and <b>Ram Khamhaeng of Sukhothai</b>; the three are said to have sworn friendship around 1287, an event commemorated by the Three Kings Monument in Chiang Mai.</blockquote><a href="/state/phayao-kingdom">Phayao Kingdom</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Sukhothai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. Died in Sukhothai, in what is now <a href="/countries/thailand">Thailand</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=17,99.82&amp;year=1276" aria-label="Sukhothai on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7265 1532 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Ram Khamhaeng"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M7715 1802h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M7715 1802h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=17,99.82&amp;year=1276">What was here in 1276? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
